  "h1": "The Sinking City 2 System Requirements",
  "seoTitle": "The Sinking City 2 System Requirements and PC Specs",
  "metaDescription": "The Sinking City 2 system requirements: minimum and recommended PC specs, Unreal Engine 5, 70 GB storage, Windows 11 x64 and SSD recommended.",
  "summary": "The Sinking City 2 system requirements target Windows 11 x64, need a 70 GB install, and run on Unreal Engine 5 with an SSD strongly recommended. The exact minimum and recommended values for CPU, GPU, RAM, and DirectX level are listed on the",
  "hero": {
    "subtitle": "Find the official minimum and recommended PC specs for The Sinking City 2 and confirm the engine.",
    "ctas": []
  },
  "quickAnswer": "The Sinking City 2 system requirements target Windows 11 x64, need a 70 GB install, and run on Unreal Engine 5 with an SSD strongly recommended. The exact minimum and recommended values for CPU, GPU, RAM, and DirectX level are listed on the Steam store page (AppID 2825860) and are the only authoritative source for those numbers. Treat any third-party \"estimated\" spec sheet as a community guess. Pre-purchase, the safest move is to compare your hardware against the live Steam page rather than against a screenshot.\n\n## The Sinking City 2 System Requirements and Where the Numbers Come From\n\nThe Sinking City 2 system requirements are published on the Steam store page for AppID 2825860. Frogwares does not maintain a separate spec sheet outside of Steam, and the PlayStation Store and Xbox Store do not publish PC spec tables. If you want the canonical minimum and recommended numbers, the only source of truth is the live Steam store page.\n\nWhy Steam-only? Steam is the only confirmed PC storefront for The Sinking City 2 as of 18 Aug 2026; there is no Epic Games Store SKU, no GOG SKU, and no standalone launcher. That makes Steam the single point of truth for PC requirements, pre-load timing, and any future day-one patch that might change the spec table. If a day-one patch tightens or relaxes the recommended spec, that change would appear first on the Steam product page.\n\nThere is also a media-attributed layer to the spec discussion. Outlets including PCGamer, Rock Paper Shotgun, wccftech, and SixthAxis have covered The Sinking City 2 alongside its review embargo, and they sometimes paraphrase the spec fields. Those paraphrases are not authoritative; only the Steam page numbers count. Use media coverage for context (engine confirmation, performance impressions) but cross-check exact values against Steam.\n\n## Engine and Platform Layer\n\nThe Sinking City 2 runs on Unreal Engine 5. Engine attribution is media-attributed (PCGamer, Rock Paper Shotgun, wccftech, SixthAxis reviews) and corroborated by the gold master build notes; the Steam store page exposes the engine dependency indirectly through the GPU and DirectX fields. Unreal Engine 5 enables the lighting, weather, and water-effects stack that defines 1920s Arkham in the game.\n\nThe game targets Windows 11 x64. That means a 64-bit Windows 11 install is the supported platform. Windows 10 64-bit is not listed as a supported platform on the Steam product page; if you are still on Windows 10, plan to upgrade to Windows 11 before installing. DirectX 12 is the expected API level for Unreal Engine 5 titles; the Steam page is the source of truth for the exact DirectX major version.\n\nThe console SKUs (PS5, Xbox Series X|S) use a separate native build path and do not share the PC spec table. Console performance is not user-tunable; you either meet the platform baseline or you do not. If you are shopping for hardware, only the PC spec table applies to your decision.\n\n## Storage, SSD, and 70 GB Install\n\nThe Sinking City 2 asks for a 70 GB install. That is the size of the launch payload on the Steam store page. After the 70 GB base install, future patches and the Premium-exclusive Holloway Manor mission add incremental storage; budget roughly 5 to 10 GB of headroom above 70 GB for post-launch updates through the first month.\n\nAn SSD is recommended, not strictly required. The minimum spec table on Steam describes the slowest supported storage tier; the recommended spec table describes the tier Frogwares tuned for during QA. Unreal Engine 5 streaming and world-partition loading rely on fast random reads for open-world sections; a hard disk drive will technically boot the game but will stutter during traversal, loading screens, and texture pop-in. For an acceptable experience, plan for an SSD as the boot drive.\n\nIf you have only an HDD and cannot add an SSD before launch, the game will still launch, but expect longer load times, texture streaming hitches, and longer cold-boot cycles. The first place a future spec relaxation or a storage optimization would be announced is the Steam news feed and the Frogwares YouTube channel. Day-one patches sometimes trim the install size by removing intermediate files; check the Steam news feed on launch day.\n\n## Minimum and Recommended PC Specs\n\nThe exact minimum and recommended PC spec values - CPU model, GPU model, RAM size, and DirectX feature level - are sourced to the Steam store page for The Sinking City 2 (AppID 2825860). Frogwares updated the spec table during the gold master lock on 7 Aug 2026; any further adjustments would also appear on the Steam page first.\n\nFor research and planning, here are the high-level fields you should expect from a Unreal Engine 5 survival-horror title in 2026: a modern quad-core or six-core CPU as the minimum, a current-generation GPU with DirectX 12 support and at least 6 GB of VRAM as the minimum, 16 GB of system RAM as the recommended baseline, and Windows 11 x64 as the only supported OS. None of those numbers are field-confirmed here; the exact strings are on the Steam page.\n\nIf you are unsure whether your PC meets the spec, the Steam client itself does a hardware check at launch time and warns you before installing if you fall under minimum. That client-side check uses the same field values as the published spec table, so it is the most reliable pre-purchase test. For platform availability and storefront notes, see the [Sinking City 2 platforms page](/platforms).\n\n## What To Upgrade First if You Are Below Recommended\n\nIf your PC meets the minimum spec but not the recommended spec, the upgrade order that has the largest visible impact on a Unreal Engine 5 survival-horror title is: SSD first, GPU second, RAM third, CPU last. SSDs affect every load and traversal hitches; the GPU affects frame rate at higher settings; RAM affects background streaming; CPU is usually the last bottleneck because UE5 scales well across modern cores.\n\nIf you are at or below the minimum spec on any one field, the game will warn you at install. That warning is conservative; many PCs slightly below minimum still run the game at lower settings. The honest move is to set in-game quality to Low or Medium, lock the framerate, and accept lower visual fidelity. The Steam spec table is the contract; treat warnings as suggestions rather than absolute blocks.\n\nIf you are pre-purchasing a new GPU or storage drive for The Sinking City 2 specifically, prioritize SSD capacity (the 70 GB install plus patch headroom) over peak GPU throughput. A mid-range current-generation GPU with an SSD outperforms a high-end GPU on a slow hard disk drive for open-world UE5 traversal. For the platform list and storefront tier per region, see the [Sinking City 2 platforms page](/platforms).",
