  "h1": "The Sinking City 2 Review Roundup",
  "seoTitle": "The Sinking City 2 Review Roundup: Critics and OpenCritic",
  "metaDescription": "The Sinking City 2 review roundup: PCGamer, wccftech, SixthAxis verdicts plus the OpenCritic Strong 81 aggregate across 40 critics as of 18 Aug 2026.",
  "summary": "The Sinking City 2 review coverage leans positive with the OpenCritic aggregate sitting at Strong, 81, top 17% across 40 critics as of 18 Aug 2026. PCGamer's Jody Macgregor calls it a \"decent survival horror / incremental improvement over t",
  "hero": {
    "subtitle": "Read a one-stop critical reception snapshot of The Sinking City 2 with the OpenCritic aggregate and named review verdicts.",
    "ctas": []
  },
  "quickAnswer": "The Sinking City 2 review coverage leans positive with the OpenCritic aggregate sitting at Strong, 81, top 17% across 40 critics as of 18 Aug 2026. PCGamer's Jody Macgregor calls it a \"decent survival horror / incremental improvement over the original\", wccftech praises it as \"eldritch survival horror done right\", and SixthAxis joins the positive camp. All reviews were embargo-locked at 17 Aug 2026 and lifted alongside the Premium Edition 24-hour early unlock. Critical consensus: a solid Cthulhu-cosmology horror sequel that builds on Frogwares' original.\n\n## The Sinking City 2 Review Aggregate on OpenCritic\n\nThe Sinking City 2 review aggregate on OpenCritic sits at \"Strong\" with a top critic average of 81 and a top 17% ranking across roughly 40 critics as of 18 Aug 2026. That places the sequel comfortably above average for survival horror releases in 2026, though short of the OpenCritic \"Mighty\" tier reserved for genre-defining work. Critics who scored in the high 70s and 80s praised the 1920s Arkham atmosphere, the Lovecraftian set pieces, and the resource-vs-avoidance combat loop; critics who docked points most often cited a sub-15-hour main story and the incremental feel versus The Sinking City (2019).\n\nIf you are weighing The Sinking City 2 against other August 2026 survival horror launches, the 81 OpenCritic average is the single most useful comparison number. Anything in the high 70s to mid 80s is a clear \"wait for a sale\" zone for cautious buyers; anything 85 and above is a \"buy at launch if the genre appeals\" signal. The Sinking City 2 sits right at the boundary. For full launch timing see the [Sinking City 2 release date page](/release-date), and for a side-by-side with the original game see [Sinking City 2 versus the original](/vs-the-sinking-city-1).\n\n## PCGamer Review Verdict\n\nPCGamer's review of The Sinking City 2 was written by Jody Macgregor and published when the embargo lifted on 17 Aug 2026. The verdict summarizes the game as \"decent survival horror\" and an \"incremental improvement over the original\" - language that signals a measured thumbs-up rather than a rave. PCGamer's positive notes lean on the Lovecraftian atmosphere, the Cal Rafferty protagonist, and the boat navigation between Arkham districts. The negative notes flag the under-15-hour main campaign length and a sense that the four-act structure is functional rather than surprising.\n\nThe PCGamer review is the most-cited English-language review of the launch window because it explicitly compares The Sinking City 2 to its 2019 predecessor and because Macgregor is one of the few reviewers who has covered Frogwares' catalog across both games. If you only read one named outlet verdict before launch, PCGamer is the one with the clearest continuity framing.\n\n## wccftech Review Verdict\n\nwccftech's review of The Sinking City 2 lands on a strongly positive verdict, headlined as \"eldritch survival horror done right\". The piece praises the Cthulhu-cosmology creature design - The Slither, Deep Ones, and Shadow enemies - and credits Frogwares for leaning into the survival-horror trade-offs rather than turning the sequel into an action shooter. wccftech also highlights the boat navigation as a structural strength, calling it the connective tissue that gives the four-act main campaign a sense of place across 1920s Arkham.\n\nWhere wccftech is more measured is on ammo economy and the resource-vs-avoidance loop: the review notes that high-difficulty players will lean more on avoidance than on gunplay, which matches the Steam-store-supported design intent. For players who prefer survival horror with a horror weight rather than a shooter weight, wccftech's verdict is the cleanest endorsement in the launch-window press.\n\n## SixthAxis Review Verdict\n\nSixthAxis reviewed The Sinking City 2 on the 17 Aug 2026 embargo lift and joined the positive camp. The SixthAxis verdict highlights the 1920s Arkham setting, the Calvin \"Cal\" Rafferty protagonist, and the side content built around Investigation missions and the boat. SixthAxis also credits the Premium Edition's Holloway Manor mission as a meaningful value add for players who picked up the top tier.\n\nSixthAxis is more reserved than wccftech on the combat design; the review treats the resource-vs-avoidance trade-off as functional rather than revelatory, which lines up with PCGamer's \"incremental\" framing. Read SixthAxis if you want a third data point confirming that the consensus is positive but not ecstatic - the kind of verdict that says \"good sequel, worth your weekend if you like the genre\".\n\n## Rock Paper Shotgun Preview Verdict\n\nRock Paper Shotgun's piece on The Sinking City 2 ran as a preview rather than a scored review. The framing is \"I've walked the waterlogged streets of The Sinking City 2, and we might finally have a Cthulhu survival horror game to rival Resident Evil and Silent Hill\". That comparison is the headline pitch: a Cthulhu survival horror with the structural weight of a mainstream Resident Evil or Silent Hill entry, rather than a Lovecraftian first-person walk in the style of the 2019 original.\n\nRock Paper Shotgun's preview is useful for buyers because it makes the closest comparison in the launch press: Resident Evil and Silent Hill are the benchmark survival horror franchises, and the preview argues The Sinking City 2 deserves to be discussed alongside them. That framing is editorially distinct from the \"incremental improvement\" language in PCGamer, and it is a useful counterweight if you are skeptical of the consensus. Read it as the most ambitious framing of the launch window, not as a final verdict.\n\n## Reviews Embargo and Reading Order\n\nReviews of The Sinking City 2 were embargoed at 17 Aug 2026, lifting at the same global unlock hour as the Premium Edition 24-hour early unlock. Standard Edition buyers could not read scored reviews before launch day, but Premium owners had review coverage live alongside their early-unlock window on 17 Aug. As of 18 Aug 2026, the full review wave has cleared embargo and OpenCritic's 40-critic count is stable.\n\nA practical reading order if you want to confirm your purchase: start with OpenCritic's aggregate page for the 81 average and the top-critic distribution, then read PCGamer for the comparison to the 2019 original, then wccftech for the strongest single endorsement, then SixthAxis for the measured third view, then Rock Paper Shotgun's preview for the most ambitious framing. For hands-on context with the first hour of the actual game, see the [Sinking City 2 getting started page](/getting-started).",
