  "h1": "The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1",
  "seoTitle": "The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1: Key Differences",
  "metaDescription": "The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1 (2019): Arkham vs Oakmont, survival horror vs open-world detective, Cal Rafferty vs Charles Reed, UE5 vs UE4.",
  "summary": "The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1 is a deliberate sequel reset, not a retread. The current title moves setting from Oakmont to Arkham, swaps Charles Reed for Cal Rafferty, shifts genre from open-world detective to semi-open survival ",
  "hero": {
    "subtitle": "Compare The Sinking City 2 against the original 2019 game across setting, genre, protagonist, mechanics, and engine.",
    "ctas": []
  },
  "quickAnswer": "The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1 is a deliberate sequel reset, not a retread. The current title moves setting from Oakmont to Arkham, swaps Charles Reed for Cal Rafferty, shifts genre from open-world detective to semi-open survival horror, replaces the 2019 sanity meter with a resource-versus-avoidance loop, and rebuilds on Unreal Engine 5. Frogwares develops both, so the change is studio-internal evolution.\n\n## The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1 Setting: Arkham Replaces Oakmont\n\nThe setting is the single biggest change in The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1. The 2019 original took place in Oakmont, a flooded 1920s American port city that Charles Reed investigated as a private detective, and the supernatural flood was the backdrop for an open-world investigation. The current title moves the action to Arkham, another 1920s Lovecraftian port city hit by the same kind of supernatural flood.\n\nBoth cities are 1920s, both are flooded, and both are framed by Cthulhu mythology, but they are not the same city. Legacy reference (The Sinking City, 2019): Oakmont is the 1920s setting Frogwares built for the 2019 release and re-released in the 2025 Remastered edition. The current Arkham is a Frogwares-built Lovecraftian port city with a different district layout and a different boat-navigation map. For the lore breakdown of the current 1920s Arkham, see the [Sinking City 2 Arkham setting page](/setting-arkham). The current setting is the frame for Cal Rafferty's survival-horror investigation rather than Charles Reed's open-world detective work.\n\n## Genre Shift: Open-World Detective to Semi-Open Survival Horror\n\nThe genre shift is the second-largest difference in The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1. The 2019 original was an open-world detective game: Charles Reed explored a freely traversable city, took cases, gathered evidence, and resolved investigations through clue-finding rather than combat. Legacy reference (The Sinking City, 2019): the 2019 loop was built around investigation and sanity management, with combat as a side activity.\n\nThe current title is a semi-open survival horror game. Cal Rafferty moves between Arkham districts by boat, the city is more constrained than the 2019 open-world map, and the four-act campaign is built around a resource economy: limited ammo, scarce healing, and a deliberate resource-versus-avoidance trade-off. Rock Paper Shotgun frames the current title as Frogwares's attempt at a Resident Evil and Silent Hill-style Cthulhu survival horror game, while wccftech calls it eldritch survival horror done right. The shift pulls the sequel from \"same series, same loop\" to \"same studio, new sub-genre,\" and it is the change the launch press leans on most. For the critical consensus, see the [Sinking City 2 review roundup](/review-roundup).\n\n## Protagonist Swap: Cal Rafferty Replaces Charles Reed\n\nThe protagonist swap is the third difference. The 2019 original starred Charles Reed, a private detective with a sanity meter whose own mind was a gameplay variable. Legacy reference (The Sinking City, 2019): Charles Reed was the war veteran investigator whose mental state was the 2019 game's signature mechanic, and he is the protagonist of the 2025 Remastered re-release.\n\nThe current title stars Calvin \"Cal\" Rafferty, a private investigator who arrives in 1920s Arkham to investigate the supernatural flood. Cal is the confirmed player character across the launch press. Faye Bennett is the confirmed supporting cast: her soul is trapped in the Dreamlands while her body has been stolen, and the Faye Bennett storyline drives the late acts. The shift from a sanity-meter-afflicted detective to a survival-horror investigator is the cleanest expression of the genre change, and it is also what disambiguates the two titles in search results. The 2019 ending coverage is kept separate from the current ending coverage because the 2019 ending structure is Charles Reed's and the current one is Cal Rafferty's. For the current ending status, see the [Sinking City 2 endings page](/endings).\n\n## Mechanics Comparison: Sanity Meter Out, Resource Economy In\n\nThe mechanics comparison is the fourth difference. The 2019 original was defined by its sanity meter, which degraded as Charles Reed encountered supernatural events. Legacy reference (The Sinking City, 2019): the sanity meter was the 2019 game's signature mechanic, and most 2019 strategy guides centered on keeping Reed's sanity from collapsing.\n\nThe current title drops the sanity meter and replaces it with a survival-horror resource economy. Ammo is scarce, healing is scarce, and the four-act campaign is built around a resource-versus-avoidance trade-off. The Slither (animates the dead), Deep Ones (surge from dark water), and Shadow enemies (bend space and test nerves) all expose a red weak point; hitting it deals critical damage and reinforces the trade-off. Base-game weapon categories are handgun, shotgun, SMG, and revolver, with Premium-exclusive Chthonic Arsenal weapons (Shale Mary, The Breakwater, Tektite Tommy) and the Premium-exclusive Holloway Manor mission adding the Bucking Bronco revolver and its attachments. The mechanics comparison is the largest player-experience change in The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1, and the one that most directly drives the 2019-versus-2026 split in strategy guides.\n\n## Engine Comparison: UE5 Replaces UE4\n\nThe engine comparison is the fifth difference. The 2019 original ran on Unreal Engine 4. Legacy reference (The Sinking City, 2019): UE4 was the 2019 base engine, and the 2025 Remastered edition rebuilt the 2019 game on a modernized UE4 pipeline rather than on UE5.\n\nThe current title runs on Unreal Engine 5 across PC (Steam AppID 2825860), PS5, and Xbox Series X|S. Frogwares Ireland Ltd. is the developer and self-publisher, and the studio's stated engine choice for the current title is UE5. The shift is the technical reason the flooded Arkham can carry higher-density lighting, denser fog volumetrics, and more detailed water effects than the 2019 Oakmont, and it is also why the survival-horror economy feels more atmospheric than the 2019 investigation loop did. It is invisible on a moment-to-moment basis, but it is the foundation the four-act Cal Rafferty campaign is built on.\n\n## Reception of the Sequel Move\n\nThe critical reception of the sequel move is the final lens for The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1. The current title launched on 18 Aug 2026 worldwide after a 17 Aug 2026 review embargo, with a Strong 81 OpenCritic aggregate across 40 critics (top 17%). PCGamer frames it as a \"decent survival horror\" and an incremental move from the 2019 formula, while Rock Paper Shotgun frames it as a genuine Cthulhu survival horror game to rival Resident Evil and Silent Hill. SixthAxis's 17 Aug 2026 review frames it as a strong return to form for Frogwares. The split is itself the clearest evidence that The Sinking City 2 vs The Sinking City 1 is a deliberate sequel reset rather than a retread. For the full critic breakdown, see the [Sinking City 2 review roundup](/review-roundup).",
